---
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro';
import '@kyralabs/m3e-css/assist-chip.css';
import '@kyralabs/m3e-css/fab.css';
---

<Guide
  title="Ripple for plain HTML"
  description="m3e-css draws the hover, focus and pressed states without JavaScript. The press ripple that spreads from the pointer needs one small import."
>
  <h2 id="import">One import</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={`import '@kyralabs/m3e-elements/ripple.js';`} lang="js" label="ripple import" />
  <p>
    It adds the ripple to every plain-HTML button, icon button, chip (assist, filter, input,
    suggestion), FAB, extended FAB, FAB menu item, <code>.m3e-state-layer</code>, interactive card,
    interactive list item, navigation item, navigation drawer item, tab, checkbox, radio button and
    switch in the document, including ones added later. One set of delegated pointer and keyboard
    listeners on <code>document</code> does it all.
  </p>
  <p>This page loads it; press these:</p>
  <div class="site-ripple-demo">
    <button type="button" class="m3e-button m3e-button--filled m3e-button--size-m">Filled</button>
    <button type="button" class="m3e-button m3e-button--tonal m3e-button--size-m">Tonal</button>
    <button type="button" class="m3e-icon-button m3e-icon-button--outlined" aria-label="Favorite">
      <span class="m3e-icon" aria-hidden="true">favorite</span>
    </button>
    <button type="button" class="m3e-assist-chip">
      <span class="m3e-icon" aria-hidden="true">event</span>
      <span class="m3e-assist-chip__label">Add to calendar</span>
    </button>
    <button type="button" class="m3e-fab" aria-label="Create">
      <span class="m3e-icon" aria-hidden="true">edit</span>
    </button>
    <article class="m3e-card m3e-card--elevated m3e-card--interactive site-ripple-demo__card">
      <h3><a class="m3e-card__action" href="#how">Interactive card</a></h3>
      <p>The whole card is the link's hit area.</p>
    </article>
  </div>

  <h2 id="how">How it behaves</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The first press on a host sets <code>data-ripple</code>, which stays; each press toggles
      <code>data-pressed</code> and writes <code>--_m3e-ripple-x</code>, <code>-y</code> and
      <code>-radius</code>, which m3e-css animates.
    </li>
    <li>
      Enter and Space press from the center. Links press on Enter only; checkboxes, filter chips,
      radio buttons and switches on Space only, since that is the key that toggles them.
    </li>
    <li>
      Hosts with a <code>data-ripple</code> it did not set are skipped: m3e-react components and
      custom elements draw their own. Disabled hosts are skipped too.
    </li>
    <li>
      Reduced motion and forced colors are handled by m3e-css: the ripple follows the motion
      tokens, and forced colors hide the overlay.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="scope">Scope it or stop it</h2>
  <p>
    <code>attachAutoRipple(root, signal)</code> from the package root does the same for one
    subtree, a shadow root or the document, without attaching to the document by itself. Aborting
    the signal removes the attributes and variables it wrote. The package root also registers every
    element, so use it where you load the elements anyway.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock
    lang="ts"
    label="attachAutoRipple example"
    code={`import { attachAutoRipple } from '@kyralabs/m3e-elements';

const controller = new AbortController();
attachAutoRipple(document.querySelector('#toolbar')!, controller.signal);
// later
controller.abort();`}
  />
  <p>
    React components and the custom elements already include their ripple; the import is only for
    markup you write with m3e-css classes.
  </p>
</Guide>
